    {
      "id": "b5acb77d-0ade-4eeb-979d-f7e922278a4f",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 353,
      "shortId": "R6E5J",
      "priority": 3,
      "shortName": "provider-gains-kind-actions-an",
      "title": "Provider gains kind(), actions() and perform() — a component declares what only it can do",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nThe trait every component that wants actions adheres to. It declares **only** what nothing else can do\n(selection-dependent semantics); everything else it wants is an existing action it *binds*.\n\n# ✅ DECIDED — `perform`, not one closure per action (user, 2026-07-29)\n\n```rust\nfn kind(&self) -> &str;                      // the TYPE — config namespace `[keys.docker]`\nfn actions(&self) -> Vec<ActionMeta>;        // declaration only; empty is a real answer\nfn perform(&self, id: &str, args: &Intent, cx: &mut ProviderCx<'_>) -> Handled;\n```\n\nWhy `perform` over `Vec<(ActionMeta, DynHandler)>`:\n\n- **It is the shape a WASM component is forced into** — an id + args crosses the boundary, a closure\n  does not. Native and plugin stay one design.\n- `&self` is available (the host is calling *into* the component); a closure outlives `actions()` and\n  must hoist every piece of state it needs into `Rc`s at registration.\n- `DynHandler` is `Fn(&mut AppState, …)` — **full mutable app state**, which §2.3 forbids a provider.\n  `ProviderCx` (read + emit) enforces the contract in the type instead of by discipline.\n- \"Which instance?\" is answered once, by the host, rather than captured per closure.\n\nThe closure form stays for the **host's own** dynamic actions (the chrome placement ones), which\nlegitimately take `&mut AppState`.\n\n# Current state\n\n- `Provider` (`heca/src/providers/mod.rs:35`): id, supported_regions, default_region, default_order,\n  title, movable, grow, collapsible, keyboard_navigable, build_contribution, context_menus,\n  on_activate. No kind, no actions.\n- `register_dynamic` (`heca/src/actions.rs:409`) + `ActionMeta` (`:2063`, carrying an unused\n  `default_binding`) + `ActionHandle` + `unregister_dynamic` all exist and are exercised by tests only.\n- `ProviderHandles` (`providers/mod.rs:100`) is documented as where the unregister handles join.\n- `WorkspacesContainerProvider::placed(id, region)` (`providers/workspaces.rs:69`) makes `id()` the\n  **mount** id — there is no `kind()`, so two placements look like two unrelated components.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. `Provider::kind() -> &str`, defaulting to `id()` for the single-placement case; workspaces returns\n   `\"workspaces\"`. Bindings and declared actions key off `kind()`; cursor/scroll/focus key off `id()`.\n2. `ProviderActions` (or the same trait) with `actions()` + `perform()`. Default `actions() = vec![]`\n   so a container that declares nothing compiles and behaves — that is the one-number-widget case.\n3. `ProviderCx<'_>`: the **mount id**, the shared store (an `Rc` alias — no borrow of `AppState`\n   held), `selected() -> Option<&str>` (this mount's cursor row), `state()` (the read-only\n   `StateView`), `dispatch(name, args)` / `emit(intent)` queued and drained by the host after\n   `perform` returns.\n4. The host bridge, written **once** for every component that will ever exist:\n   ```rust\n   fn route_to_owner(state: &mut AppState, intent: &Intent) {\n       let Some(mount) = owning_mount(state, &intent.action) else { return };\n       let store = state.chrome_state.clone();\n       let mut cx = ProviderCx::new(&mount, store, state.intent_sink());\n       if let Some(p) = state.chrome_host.provider(&mount) { p.perform(&intent.action, intent, &mut cx); }\n       cx.drain_into(state);\n   }\n   ```\n   Borrow note: the provider lives *inside* `state.chrome_host`, so no `&mut AppState` may be alive\n   during `perform` — `ProviderCx` holds only clones and a queue.\n5. `owning_mount`: the focused mount if it owns the id, else the **last-focused instance of that kind**,\n   else the first mounted. Written down once, not decided per call site.\n6. Register on mount / retire on unmount: `on_activate` returns the declared metas in\n   `ProviderHandles`; the **host** calls `register_dynamic` (a provider never mutates app state) and\n   `unregister_dynamic` on unmount.\n7. `ActionMeta.default_binding` stops being dead code — consumed by the config task.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- Re-registering the same id on remount replaces the previous entry (already the documented behaviour).\n- Two placements of one kind share declared actions and bindings; `perform` is called on the placement\n  `owning_mount` picked, and reads *its* cursor.\n- `perform` returning `Handled::No` (nothing selected) is a legitimate silent decline; a later\n  `can_perform` could grey out a menu entry — do NOT build it now.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- A component declares two actions and a five-line `perform`, and gets keys, RPC, palette, context menu\n  and the confirm gate from that alone.\n- No component can reach `&mut AppState`.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:22:41.503Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-28T12:22:41.503Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "a556de02-39e0-4664-91b0-304d47db75d5",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 354,
      "shortId": "76N2N",
      "priority": 4,
      "shortName": "nav-key-a-row-declares-its-ide",
      "title": "nav_key: a row declares its identity once — cursor, right-click target, later drag",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nOne declaration on a row, three readers. A component labels its rows; the host derives the keyboard\ncursor, the context-menu `(path, target)`, and (later) the drag identity from it. No app-side enum of\nrow kinds anywhere.\n\n# Current state — three closed enums doing this job\n\n- **Right-click**: `mouse.rs` hit-tests the sidebar into a `ChromeDragItem`\n  (`Pane | Column | Workspace` — a closed domain enum) and `open_sidebar_context_menu`\n  (`heca/src/mouse.rs:143`) **matches on it** to produce `(ContextPath, ContextTarget)`. `ContextTarget`\n  is closed too (`Pane / SidebarPane / SidebarColumn / SidebarWorkspace`). A plugin row cannot be\n  expressed, so a `container.docker` path could never be produced — the contribution is filtered out\n  and the menu is empty.\n- **Keyboard menu**: `resolve_active_context` (`chrome/context_menu.rs:308`) reads\n  `InputMode::SidebarNav` + the sidebar cursor — a second, keyboard-specific resolution of the same\n  fact.\n- **Cursor**: `SidebarTree.cursor` + `WorkspacesContainerState.nav_selection`, re-projected after every\n  rebuild by `apply_nav_selection` — correct in shape (identity, not an index) but domain-typed.\n\nThe precedent to copy is the hint registry: rows already register *the intent a click fires*\n(`hints.register(InteractionIntent::FocusPane { pane_id })`, `providers/workspaces.rs:201`) and\n`collect_hint_targets` (`heca-grid-ui/src/hint.rs:45`) walks a subtree in document order.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. **Library**: `Base.nav_key: Option<…>` + a builder (`.nav_key(\"abc\")`), and a collector that walks a\n   subtree in document order returning `(nav_key, bounds)` — the hint collector's twin (or the same\n   walk, extended). Rustdoc + `docs/widgets.md`.\n2. **Cursor, per mount**: `container_cursor: HashMap<mount, Option<NavKey>>` in `SharedChromeState`,\n   beside `container_scroll` and `focused_container`. Survives a tree rebuild because it is an identity,\n   not an index — this is why a scoped `FocusManager` (a `usize` visit index, `heca-grid-ui/src/focus.rs:50`)\n   cannot be the cursor.\n3. **Highlight**: generalise `ChromeSignals.pane_nav`/`ws_nav` (domain-keyed today) to\n   `(mount, NavKey, Signal<bool>)`, driven from the cursor in `sync_chrome_signals`. `Row`/`Item`/\n   `DockFrame`/`MarkerGroup` already expose `nav_state()`.\n4. **Scroll-into-view**: a cursor row overrides `wants_visible()` (`component.rs:543` — documented for\n   exactly \"a list's navigation cursor\"), so the enclosing `ScrollRegion` follows it with nothing wired.\n5. **Right-click resolution moves to the tree**: walk the retained chrome tree at the cursor,\n   innermost-first (as `drag::resolve_at` and `collect_hint_targets` already do), take the first node\n   carrying a `nav_key`, and produce `path = container.<kind>` (derived from the owning component) +\n   `ContextTarget::Row { container: mount, key }`. An optional per-row `.context_path(..)` override\n   covers a component with several row kinds in one body.\n6. **`ContextTarget` gains the open `Row` variant** and `row_key()`. The closed variants stay until the\n   workspaces migration (next task) removes them.\n7. **`resolve_active_context` stops naming SidebarNav**: the focused container + its cursor produce the\n   same `(path, target)` the pointer path produces. `prefix+>` and right-click then resolve identically\n   for every component.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- Two placements of one component: cursors are per mount (this is the T021 lesson — scroll offsets\n  keyed by kind made two placements move together, invisible until there were two).\n- A rebuild between the cursor being set and read: re-project by key; a key that no longer exists clears\n  the cursor rather than pointing at a neighbour.\n- Rows that are not navigable simply carry no `nav_key`.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- One declaration per row; the cursor, the context menu and the keyboard menu all read it.\n- No `ChromeDragItem` match in the context-menu path.\n- A plugin row can be right-clicked and its component's entries appear.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:24:11.990Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-28T12:24:11.990Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "b1e0e7b5-b372-40bd-89be-fd3343729474",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 355,
      "shortId": "NH962",
      "priority": 5,
      "shortName": "keys-lt-kind-gt-a-component-s",
      "title": "Per-kind keybindings: a component's bindings, merged by key, live only while it is focused",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nPer-component keymaps: shipped defaults, user-overridable one key at a time, resolved only while that\ncomponent holds chrome focus.\n\n# Current state\n\n- The keymap is built from config **at load** (`build_keymap`, `heca/src/app/registry.rs`), before any\n  provider activates — so a declared `ActionMeta.default_binding` (`actions.rs:2072`,\n  `#[allow(dead_code)]`) reaches nothing today.\n- Config merge rules (AGENTS § Config Loading): tables merge **per key**; scalars and **arrays are\n  replaced wholesale**. That is why `[[keys.mode]]` blocks have to be merged back by name at runtime\n  (`build_modes`) — a user redefining one mode would otherwise lose the built-ins.\n- `action_ref_from_config(name, args)` already resolves a name to `ActionRef::Builtin` **or**\n  `ActionRef::Dynamic` at press, so binding a component's own id needs no new resolution.\n- `[keys.unbind]` already removes a binding **by key combo**, \"whatever it points at\".\n\n# ✅ DECIDED — the shape\n\n```toml\n[keys.docker]                 # a TABLE: merges per key, so overriding one keeps the rest\nrestart_selected = \"r\"        # the component's own action (its kind namespaces it)\nstop_selected    = \"s\"\nfocus_next_pane  = \"n\"        # ← an EXISTING built-in, just bound here\n\n[[keys.docker.bind]]          # the arg-carrying form — arrays are otherwise replaced wholesale,\naction = \"spawn_command\"      #   so these merge by their `keys` field (a keymap IS a map from\nkeys   = \"t\"                  #   combo to action; merging on the combo is not a special case)\nargs   = { command = \"lazydocker\", float = \"true\" }\n\n[keys.docker.unbind]          # explicit removal — NOT null/empty-string semantics (the program\n\"s\" = true                    #   catalog uses `disabled = true` for the same reason)\n```\n\n# Steps\n\n1. Parse `[keys.<kind>]` (table), `[[keys.<kind>.bind]]` (array, merged by `keys`) and\n   `[keys.<kind>.unbind]` in `heca-config` beside the existing `[keys]`/`[[keys.mode]]` handling.\n2. Merge-by-key for the array form: a user entry with the same `keys` replaces the default's; everything\n   else survives. This is the fix that makes the arg-carrying form usable at all.\n3. **Bind declared defaults at mount** (`bind_component_default`): after a provider's actions are\n   registered, bind each `ActionMeta.default_binding` into that kind's layer **unless the user already\n   bound that key or rebound that action id** — user config always wins, and a default never silently\n   shadows one.\n4. Resolution: while a component holds chrome focus, its layer is consulted (see the routing task).\n   Built-in components ship their defaults in `keybindings.default.toml` so the defaults stay\n   discoverable (AGENTS: a default that lives only in code is a bug); a **plugin** has no line in that\n   file, so its manifest is the source there, with user config still winning.\n5. Document both in `README.md` and `keybindings.default.toml`, including the merge-by-key rule and\n   `unbind`.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- A binding to an id whose component is not mounted is **not** an error (config is read before\n  providers register — the existing `ActionRef::Dynamic` rule): it simply does nothing until the\n  component appears.\n- Two placements of one kind share the layer (bindings belong to the type; only state is per mount).\n- A component that declares no actions and binds nothing needs no table at all.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- `[keys.docker] stop_selected = \"x\"` changes one key and keeps `r` and `t`.\n- `[[keys.docker.bind]]` with `keys = \"t\"` replaces exactly that default and keeps the others.\n- `ActionMeta.default_binding` is no longer dead code.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:24:35.383Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-28T12:27:19.592Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "3555aed0-93d6-4b19-9e69-ba7ce2771657",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 356,
      "shortId": "F3J4W",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "migrate-the-workspaces-contain",
      "title": "Migrate the workspaces container onto the contract; delete SidebarNav and the sidebar_* built-ins",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nMake the first-party container the first consumer of the contract, and remove the bespoke mode it\nreplaces. This is the task that proves the design: if the workspace tree cannot be expressed as a\ncomponent that declares its actions, the design is wrong.\n\n# What is being deleted\n\n- `InputMode::SidebarNav` (`heca/src/app_state.rs:58`) + `handle_sidebar_nav_mode`\n  (`heca/src/app/input.rs:584`) + `restorable_mode` (`chrome/context_menu.rs:334`, whose only arm is\n  `SidebarNav`).\n- The 11 `sidebar_*` built-ins: `sidebar_focus`, `sidebar_up`, `sidebar_down`, `sidebar_left_nav`,\n  `sidebar_right_nav`, `sidebar_peek`, `sidebar_expand_toggle`, `sidebar_create_workspace`,\n  `sidebar_create_column`, `sidebar_split_in_column`, `sidebar_zoom_selected_column`,\n  `sidebar_delete_selected` — their `WmAction` variants, `action_from_name` arms, `action_priority`\n  arms, handlers (`handlers.rs:1562+`), registrations (`app/registry.rs:659+`), descriptors, RPC\n  commands and the `[[keys.mode]] name = \"sidebar\"` block.\n- `SidebarTree.cursor` as the nav cursor (the tree stays as the *projection* the body is built from;\n  the cursor becomes the generic per-mount one).\n- `state.chrome_state.workspaces.nav_selection` → the generic `container_cursor` +\n  `ContainerSelectionChanged`.\n\n# What replaces it\n\n`WorkspacesContainerProvider` declares:\n\n- `kind() = \"workspaces\"` (bindings) vs `id()` = the mount (cursor, scroll, focus).\n- **Declared actions** — only the selection-dependent ones nothing else can do:\n  `workspaces.activate_selected` (focus the pane/workspace under the cursor — today's\n  `sidebar_right_nav`/Enter), `workspaces.peek_selected` (focus but stay), `workspaces.toggle_selected`\n  (expand/collapse the row), `workspaces.rename_selected`, `workspaces.delete_selected`,\n  `workspaces.create_workspace`, `workspaces.create_column`, `workspaces.split_selected`,\n  `workspaces.zoom_selected`. Each with label, icon, `policy`, and `confirm` where destructive — the\n  central gate then prompts on every surface, which is what `sidebar_delete_selected` does by hand\n  today.\n- **Bindings it does not declare**: the cursor moves (`j`/`k`/arrows) act on its own rows, so they are\n  its actions too — but anything already in the catalog (`zoom_column`, `close`, `next_pane`) is simply\n  **bound**, not redeclared.\n- `perform` resolves the cursor (`cx.selected()`) to a `SidebarItem` and dispatches the existing\n  parameterized actions (`FocusPane`, `RenamePaneById`, `DeleteWorkspace`, …) — which is where the\n  domain knowledge belongs and is exactly what `handle_sidebar_right_nav` does today from the wrong\n  place.\n- Defaults shipped as `[keys.workspaces]` in `keybindings.default.toml`, preserving today's letters\n  (`j`/`k`/`h`/`l`/`Enter`/`Space`/`Tab`/`w`/`c`/`v`/`z`/`d`) so muscle memory survives.\n\n`sidebar_focus` (`prefix+e`) becomes: focus the navigable dock (already implemented, F003/P011/T020) —\nthe mode it used to enter no longer exists, because focus **is** the mode.\n\n# Behaviours to preserve (they were settled with /grill-me; do not casually re-decide)\n\nFrom AGENTS § \"Sidebar Phase 1.5 semantic rules already settled\": navigation is selection-driven; the\ncursor does not move main focus; `h`/`l` are tree navigation on structural rows; `Enter`/`l`/`Right`\nactivate **and leave**, `Space` peeks **and stays**; `Esc` exits and focuses contextual content;\nmutation keys are container-only; the global collapse actions act on the **active main-view** state,\nnot the cursor.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. Declare the actions + `perform`; keep the existing handler bodies, called from `perform` with an\n   explicit target instead of reading `state.sidebar_tree.current_item()`.\n2. `.nav_key()` on pane cards, column groups and workspace docks (`pane:<id>`, `col:<ws>:<idx>`,\n   `ws:<idx>`) — the same identities `nav_selection` uses today.\n3. Move the context-menu paths to `container.workspaces` + `ContextTarget::Row`, and delete the\n   `ChromeDragItem` match in `open_sidebar_context_menu` (`mouse.rs:143`).\n4. Delete everything listed above; update `README.md` (the sidebar-mode tables), `keybindings.default.toml`,\n   `docs/chrome-and-ui.md` §2.10.\n5. Tests: every preserved behaviour above, plus \"the workspaces dock declares no action that duplicates\n   an existing built-in\".\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- `prefix+Shift+e` → pick the dock → `j`/`k`/`Enter`/`d` work, unprefixed, exactly as `prefix+e` used to.\n- No `SidebarNav`, no `sidebar_*` action, no domain enum in the menu/nav path.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings. **The USER drives it in the app.**",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:25:04.138Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-28T12:25:04.138Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "893b625c-4818-4fd3-be49-8776303e8ecf",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 357,
      "shortId": "5BB37",
      "priority": 7,
      "shortName": "hooks-what-is-focused-what-is",
      "title": "Hooks: what is focused, what is selected, what was dispatched",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nMake the three facts a plugin needs observable from the shared store + event bus, so a component (or a\nfuture WASM plugin) can react to focus, selection and action dispatch without polling or touching\n`AppState`. Requested by the user 2026-07-29: *\"the global state AppState should be aware of which item\nis focused, which action is being dispatched, so that plugins can hook into\"*.\n\n# Current state\n\n- **Focus**: `SharedChromeState.focused_container` + `ChromeEvent::ContainerFocusChanged` — landed\n  (F003/P011/T020).\n- **Selection**: only the workspaces container has one, domain-typed:\n  `WorkspacesContainerState.nav_selection` + `ChromeEvent::SidebarSelectionChanged`\n  (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs:171`, `events.rs`). It is exactly the right mechanism for one hardcoded\n  container.\n- **Dispatch**: there is already a single chokepoint — `dispatch_action` / `dispatch_intent` →\n  `route_interaction` → `registry.execute` (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs`) — and nothing is emitted from\n  it.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. `ChromeEvent::ContainerSelectionChanged { container, key: Option<NavKey> }` — the generalisation of\n   `SidebarSelectionChanged`, emitted by the per-mount cursor setter (see the `nav_key` task). Retire\n   `SidebarSelectionChanged` with the workspaces migration.\n2. `ChromeEvent::ActionDispatched { name, args, source }` emitted at the chokepoint, **after** the\n   policy allows and **before** execution.\n3. **It is a notification, not a veto** (decided 2026-07-29). A plugin cancelling a dispatch is a much\n   larger contract — ordering, who wins, what a partial cancel means — and it can be added later\n   without changing this. Do not build a veto.\n4. Expose the reads on the host facade (`heca/src/host.rs` `StateView`): `focused_container()` (landed),\n   `container_selection(mount)`, so a provider reads them through the same facade it already uses for\n   `container_scroll` / `container_keyboard_target`.\n5. Document the three events in `docs/chrome-and-ui.md` beside §2.10.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- Emit only on real change (every setter in this store is change-guarded; keep it that way, or a\n  per-frame sync will spam subscribers).\n- `ActionDispatched` must not fire for a **blocked** action — the interaction policy runs first, and an\n  action the router refused never happened.\n- Beware re-entrancy: a subscriber that dispatches an action from inside the handler. Document the rule\n  (subscribers observe; they queue intents rather than dispatching synchronously) and cover it with a\n  test.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- A subscriber sees focus, selection and dispatch without reading `AppState`.\n- No veto surface.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:25:20.627Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-28T12:25:20.627Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "95ef84f9-7def-4c4e-8032-f87d583faac8",
      "phaseId": "5de43f15-8304-4f64-870f-8514d534d5a5",
      "number": 358,
      "shortId": "ABFAU",
      "priority": 8,
      "shortName": "palette-and-rpc-reach-a-compon",
      "title": "Palette and RPC reach a component's actions from anywhere: FocusContainerThenAction",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What\n\nA declared action must be usable without first focusing its component by hand — from the command\npalette, from RPC, from a menu — while still meaning something (a selection-dependent action needs its\ncomponent to be the focused one).\n\n# ✅ DECIDED (user question 2026-07-29: \"what will the command palette discover?\")\n\n**Everything registered, and registration follows mounting.** A component's actions enter the catalog\non mount and are retired with it (`ProviderHandles` + `unregister_dynamic`), so an action for a\ncomponent that is not mounted simply does not exist — no filtering needed, it falls out.\n\n**Mounted but unfocused: still shown.** Choosing one focuses its component and then performs, using the\nprecedent already in the codebase for exactly this problem: pane-header buttons whose action needs the\npane focused register `InteractionIntent::FocusPaneThenAction { pane_id, action }`, which\n`dispatch_intent` expands into focus-then-act (`heca/src/app/interaction.rs`, and see AGENTS §\n\"Active-targeted buttons must focus first\").\n\n# Steps\n\n1. `InteractionIntent::FocusContainerThenAction { container, action }`, expanded in `dispatch_intent`\n   into `FocusDock { dock } ` + the action — mirroring `FocusPaneThenAction` exactly.\n2. Palette entries for component actions carry it, and are labelled with their scope:\n   `\"Containers › Restart Container\"` — the component's `title()` + the action's `label`, both already\n   in metadata. The focused component's actions sort first; nothing is hidden.\n3. **Which instance**, when a kind is mounted twice: the last-focused instance of that kind, else the\n   first mounted. One rule, written down once (`owning_mount`, shared with the `perform` bridge) rather\n   than decided per call site.\n4. RPC: the same resolution, plus an explicit override for scripts — `--dock docker.right` — so a script\n   can target a specific placement without focusing it first.\n5. `list-actions` / `describe-action` include component actions with their owner, so introspection shows\n   who owns what.\n\n# Edge cases\n\n- A selection-dependent action chosen from the palette with nothing selected: `perform` declines\n  silently after focusing. Acceptable for now; a later optional `can_perform(&self, id) -> bool` would\n  drive a disabled entry (menu entries already have an enabled state). **Do not build it now.**\n- An action id whose component was unmounted between the palette opening and the choice: resolve to\n  nothing and say so, rather than dispatching into the void.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- Searching the palette finds `Restart Container` while the Docker dock is mounted and unfocused;\n  choosing it focuses that dock and restarts.\n- `heca action docker.stop_selected` works from a script, with `--dock` to disambiguate two placements.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "",
      "completedAt": "",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-28T12:25:38.894Z",
